  "concerns": [
    {
      "id": "lem-highest-weight-vectors-in-a-schur-tensor-product-are-lr-tableaux",
      "concern": "The Macdonald source locator says that the proof of (9.4) identifies lattice-condition tableaux with the 'recursive/dominant-weight description'. In the opened complete §I.9, printed pp. 142–148, (9.4) instead bijects arbitrary skew tableaux with pairs consisting of an LR tableau and an ordinary tableau. It proves the lattice-word Schur coefficient formula (9.2), which supports this item's coefficient-comparison step 4.1; it does not directly state the column-admissible comparison. The local argument remains supported, but the locator overstates the source's direct content. Evidence: https://math.berkeley.edu/~corteel/MATH249/macdonald.pdf, equations (9.2)–(9.4), printed p. 143; compare the local proof's steps 3.1–4.1. Stembridge's complete four-page proof was also opened and matches the local admissible-tableau involution.",
      "severity": "presentation"
    }
  ],
  "alerts": [
    {
      "item": "thm-weak-global-w-two-p-dirichlet-regularity-for-the-laplacian",
      "finding": "F1 says 'for the Laplacian the Dirichlet form is a(v,φ)=∫_Ω ∇v·∇φ' while citing def-weak-dirichlet-solution-for-a-divergence-form-operator. The cited convention is a(v,φ)=∫_Ω ∇v·overline(∇φ), and the consumer does not restrict to real scalars; F6 explicitly discusses complex conjugation. For v=φ=iη with nonzero real compactly supported η, the two named forms have opposite diagonal values. The quantified bilinear weak equations are equivalent to the sesquilinear equations by replacing φ with its conjugate, so this is a convention/citation presentation defect, not a counterexample to the regularity statement.",
      "severity": "presentation"
    }
  ],
